%% CANARY for rule 1 where the crossing edge is written with mermaid's multi-target `&`.
%% `x --> y & z` is TWO links, not one, and the corpus really writes it that way — see
%% platform-orientation/02-platform-accretion-v1.mmd's `DEP --> POD1 & POD2 & POD3`. A reader that
%% takes only the first id off the segment sees `outside --> other`, both of them outside the box,
%% and reports nothing at all while the second target reaches straight into it.
%% EXPECT: ignored-direction | - — baseline only. Every pattern is owned by another fixture; what
%% this one owns is that a segment between two link operators can name more than one node.
flowchart TB
%% MUST-FIRE
  subgraph A["left box"]
    direction LR
    a1["one"] --> a2["two"]
  end
  outside["elsewhere"] --> other["also outside"] & a1
